`results/solution_report.md` — verified UTF-8, 397 lines, all required sections, **text-only (no images/plots/data-URLs)**, self-contained.

### What the model does
A triple-bottom-line (economic–social–environmental) welfare model over Juneau's 200-day season: concave visitor benefit − convex carbon, congestion and site-degradation costs − public-service cost, with resident disamenity weighted by an equity factor. It yields closed-form efficient load and Pigouvian fee, simulates four policies to 2035, allocates new revenue, runs OAT + Sobol′ sensitivity, and generalises to other destinations.

### Anchors used (2 verified empirical items)
1. **CBJ/Southeast Conference cruise-impact report (2024)** — $375 M direct spending, $22.3 M port revenue, 3,850 jobs.
2. **The 16,000/12,000 cap + 5-ship MOA** (Juneau Empire / ABC7 / CBJ MOA).

**Calibration validated:** model reproduces 1.67 M passengers, $375 M spending, $22.3 M revenue, and peak-day load 20,547 vs. reported "≤20,000".

### Headline results
| Result | Value |
|---|---|
| Efficient peak-day load | **≈15,800/day** — confirms the negotiated 16,000 cap within 1.3 % |
| Efficient marine-passenger fee | **≈$137/pax** vs current **$5** (≈4 % of the true external cost) |
| Pure monetised optimum (λ_r =1) | 33,085/day → overtourism = classic unpriced externality |
| 10-yr discounted welfare | S0 $535 M < S1 $642 M < S2 $710 M < **S3 integrated $940 M** |
| 2035 (integrated vs status quo) | carbon −36 %, wellbeing 0.79 vs 0.59, welfare +113 % |
| New revenue / reinvestment | **$222 M/yr** across infrastructure 25 %, transit 22 %, housing 20 %, environment 18 %, shore power 10 %, dispersal 5 % |

**Core message:** caps control *volume*, fees raise *money* — only the integrated package wins on every metric; the single most sensitive factors are visitor value and the social cost of carbon (Sobol′ ≈ 0.41 and 0.39).

